    {
      "stem": "How do MSE, Mean Absolute Error (MAE), and Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE) differ in calculation, units, and sensitivity to large errors?",
      "tag": "Medium · Related metrics",
      "answer": "c",
      "options": [
        "MAE averages squared errors and is measured in squared target units, while MSE averages absolute errors in original units. RMSE is the square root of MAE, which limits unusual errors.",
        "MAE, MSE, and RMSE each average absolute errors with a different constant multiplier. Their units stay the same, and they give large mistakes nearly identical importance during evaluation.",
        "MAE averages absolute errors in original units. MSE averages squared errors in squared units. RMSE takes the square root of MSE, returning to original units; both MSE and RMSE emphasize large misses.",
        "MAE averages absolute errors, MSE averages squared errors, and RMSE divides the MSE result by the sample count again. This makes RMSE much less sensitive to large mistakes than MAE."
      ],
      "explanation": "<strong>Explanation:</strong> MAE averages absolute errors, MSE averages their squares, and RMSE takes the square root of MSE. MAE and RMSE have the original target units; MSE has squared units. MSE and RMSE give larger misses more influence than MAE. For delivery-delay errors of 0, 1, and 3 minutes, MAE is 1.33 minutes, MSE is 3.33 minutes², and RMSE is about 1.83 minutes. Compare errors only on a compatible target scale.",
      "figure": "<section class=\"mse-figure\"><span class=\"mse-kicker\">QUESTION 04 · VISUAL LESSON</span><div class=\"mse-art\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 440\" role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"mse-title-4 mse-desc-4\"><title id=\"mse-title-4\">MAE, MSE and RMSE are not interchangeable</title><desc id=\"mse-desc-4\">For errors 0 1 3 minutes, MAE is 1.33 minutes, MSE is 3.33 minutes squared, and RMSE is 1.83 minutes.</desc><rect width=\"1000\" height=\"440\" fill=\"#f8fcfd\"/><text x=\"38\" y=\"39\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-size=\"14\" font-family=\"system-ui,Arial,sans-serif\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#278876\">04 · MEAN SQUARED ERROR</text><text x=\"38\" y=\"79\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-size=\"25\" font-family=\"system-ui,Arial,sans-serif\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#2e5368\">MAE, MSE and RMSE are not interchangeable</text><rect x=\"36\" y=\"115\" width=\"930\" height=\"259\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#d5e5e9\" stroke-width=\"1.4\"/><text x=\"59\" y=\"153\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-size=\"18\" font-family=\"system-ui,Arial,sans-serif\" font-weight=\"830\" fill=\"#547f90\">SAME ERRORS:  0, 1, 3 MINUTES</text><rect x=\"58\" y=\"174\" width=\"267\" height=\"171\" rx=\"13\" fill=\"#ecf8fb\" stroke=\"#d7e5e9\" stroke-width=\"1.4\"/><text x=\"78\" y=\"211\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-size=\"20\" font-family=\"system-ui,Arial,sans-serif\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#377f99\">MAE</text><text x=\"78\" y=\"247\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-size=\"18\" font-family=\"system-ui,Arial,sans-serif\" font-weight=\"730\" fill=\"#456d7f\">(0 + 1 + 3) / 3</text><text x=\"78\" y=\"289\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-size=\"26\" font-family=\"system-ui,Arial,sans-serif\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#377f99\">1.33 min</text><text x=\"78\" y=\"326\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-size=\"16\" font-family=\"system-ui,Arial,sans-serif\" font-weight=\"640\" fill=\"#617e8c\">Absolute errors</text><rect x=\"368\" y=\"174\" width=\"267\" height=\"171\" rx=\"13\" fill=\"#edf9f0\" stroke=\"#d7e5e9\" stroke-width=\"1.4\"/><text x=\"388\" y=\"211\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-size=\"20\" font-family=\"system-ui,Arial,sans-serif\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#298a7c\">MSE</text><text x=\"388\" y=\"247\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-size=\"18\" font-family=\"system-ui,Arial,sans-serif\" font-weight=\"730\" fill=\"#456d7f\">(0 + 1 + 9) / 3</text><text x=\"388\" y=\"289\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-size=\"26\" font-family=\"system-ui,Arial,sans-serif\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#298a7c\">3.33 min²</text><text x=\"388\" y=\"326\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-size=\"16\" font-family=\"system-ui,Arial,sans-serif\" font-weight=\"640\" fill=\"#617e8c\">Squared errors</text><rect x=\"678\" y=\"174\" width=\"267\" height=\"171\" rx=\"13\" fill=\"#fff3e9\" stroke=\"#d7e5e9\" stroke-width=\"1.4\"/><text x=\"698\" y=\"211\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-size=\"20\" font-family=\"system-ui,Arial,sans-serif\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#b57a52\">RMSE</text><text x=\"698\" y=\"247\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-size=\"18\" font-family=\"system-ui,Arial,sans-serif\" font-weight=\"730\" fill=\"#456d7f\">√(10 / 3)</text><text x=\"698\" y=\"289\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-size=\"26\" font-family=\"system-ui,Arial,sans-serif\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#b57a52\">1.83 min</text><text x=\"698\" y=\"326\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-size=\"16\" font-family=\"system-ui,Arial,sans-serif\" font-weight=\"640\" fill=\"#617e8c\">Square-root MSE</text><text x=\"61\" y=\"403\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-size=\"16\" font-family=\"system-ui,Arial,sans-serif\" font-weight=\"690\" fill=\"#5d7e8d\">Caution: squared minutes and minutes are different units.</text></svg></div><p class=\"mse-note\">MAE and RMSE have original units; MSE has squared target units.</p></section>"
    },
    {
      "stem": "How can MSE be calculated using Python and Scikit-learn on held-out regression predictions, and why should preprocessing be fitted only on training data?",
      "tag": "Medium · Python evaluation",
      "answer": "b",
      "options": [
        "Use mean_squared_error with training labels and fitted training predictions after scaling every example before splitting. This provides a reliable unseen-data MSE because squaring makes any preprocessing leakage unimportant.",
        "Split regression data first, then fit preprocessing and the model using only training examples. Predict held-out values, calculate mean_squared_error(y_test, predictions), and optionally verify the result by manually averaging squared errors.",
        "Split regression data first, fit a separate scaler on the held-out test targets, and calculate mean_squared_error on those rescaled values. Independent test scaling prevents leakage and makes all target errors directly comparable.",
        "Train a regression model using every available labeled example, then report mean_squared_error on the same examples. Agreement with a manual calculation proves the score estimates new-data performance without a holdout."
      ],
      "explanation": "<strong>Explanation:</strong> Split the dataset before fitting preprocessing. Fit your scaler and regressor only on training examples, predict held-out targets, then calculate mean_squared_error(y_test, predictions). A NumPy check using the average of squared differences should match. The included synthetic Scikit-learn example produces about 106.218 MSE with fixed random seeds. For museum attendance forecasting, learning normalization values from future held-out records would leak information and could make reported evaluation misleading.",
      "figure": "<section class=\"mse-figure\"><span class=\"mse-kicker\">QUESTION 05 · VISUAL LESSON</span><div class=\"mse-art\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 440\" role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"mse-title-5 mse-desc-5\"><title id=\"mse-title-5\">Fit on training data. Evaluate on held-out cases.</title><desc id=\"mse-desc-5\">Split before fitting; Ridge on standardized training features predicts sixty held-out cases and MSE is about 106.218.</desc><rect width=\"1000\" height=\"440\" fill=\"#f8fcfd\"/><text x=\"38\" y=\"39\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-size=\"14\" font-family=\"system-ui,Arial,sans-serif\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#278876\">05 · MEAN SQUARED ERROR</text><text x=\"38\" y=\"79\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-size=\"25\" font-family=\"system-ui,Arial,sans-serif\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#2e5368\">Fit on training data. Evaluate on held-out cases.</text><rect x=\"31\" y=\"111\" width=\"936\" height=\"260\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#d5e5e9\" stroke-width=\"1.4\"/><rect x=\"54\" y=\"172\" width=\"183\" height=\"116\" rx=\"13\" fill=\"#ebf7fc\" stroke=\"#d6e6e9\" stroke-width=\"1.4\"/><text x=\"145.5\" y=\"209\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"18\" font-family=\"system-ui,Arial,sans-serif\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#3a8196\">TRAIN</text><text x=\"145.5\" y=\"251\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"17\" font-family=\"system-ui,Arial,sans-serif\" font-weight=\"660\" fill=\"#4f7384\">180 cases</text><rect x=\"339\" y=\"172\" width=\"242\" height=\"116\" rx=\"13\" fill=\"#f2f4ff\" stroke=\"#d6e6e9\" stroke-width=\"1.4\"/><text x=\"460\" y=\"209\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"18\" font-family=\"system-ui,Arial,sans-serif\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#6174a2\">FIT PIPELINE</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"251\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"17\" font-family=\"system-ui,Arial,sans-serif\" font-weight=\"660\" fill=\"#4f7384\">StandardScaler + Ridge</text><rect x=\"668\" y=\"172\" width=\"264\" height=\"116\" rx=\"13\" fill=\"#e7f8ee\" stroke=\"#d6e6e9\" stroke-width=\"1.4\"/><text x=\"800\" y=\"209\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"18\" font-family=\"system-ui,Arial,sans-serif\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#2d8c77\">TEST + MSE</text><text x=\"800\" y=\"251\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"17\" font-family=\"system-ui,Arial,sans-serif\" font-weight=\"660\" fill=\"#4f7384\">60 cases · 106.218</text><path d=\"M240 231 H330 M318 221 L330 231 L318 241\" stroke=\"#6a9fa8\" stroke-width=\"3\" fill=\"none\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><path d=\"M591 231 H660 M648 221 L660 231 L648 241\" stroke=\"#6a9fa8\" stroke-width=\"3\" fill=\"none\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><text x=\"57\" y=\"337\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-size=\"20\" font-family=\"system-ui,Arial,sans-serif\" font-weight=\"720\" fill=\"#426e80\">Keep test data unseen while choosing transformations and model settings.</text></svg></div><div class=\"mse-code\"><div class=\"mse-code-top\">Python · 14 runnable Scikit-learn lines</div><pre style=\"background:#1e1e1e;color:#f0f8ff;white-space:pre;overflow-x:auto\"><code style=\"display:block;background:transparent!important;color:#f0f8ff!important;border:0;padding:0;white-space:pre;font:inherit\">from sklearn.datasets import make_regression\nfrom sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split\nfrom sklearn.pipeline import make_pipeline\nfrom sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler\nfrom sklearn.linear_model import Ridge\nfrom sklearn.metrics import mean_squared_error\nimport numpy as np\nX,y = make_regression(n_samples=240,n_features=4,noise=12,random_state=17)\nXtr,Xte,ytr,yte = train_test_split(X,y,test_size=.25,random_state=42)\nmodel = make_pipeline(StandardScaler(),Ridge(alpha=1.0))\nmodel.fit(Xtr,ytr)\npred = model.predict(Xte)\nprint(round(mean_squared_error(yte,pred),3))\nprint(np.mean((yte-pred)**2))</code></pre></div><p class=\"mse-note\">Held-out testing prevents optimistic reuse of the training observations.</p></section>"
    },
    {
      "stem": "How does MSE influence gradient-based training of regression models, including its derivative, the effect of large errors, and optimization stability?",
      "tag": "Advanced · Optimization",
      "answer": "d",
      "options": [
        "MSE is smooth, but its gradient with respect to each prediction is always 2/n regardless of mistake size. Large errors therefore influence learning no more than small errors during gradient-based training.",
        "MSE uses the absolute value of prediction error divided by n as its derivative. This forces every gradient to point in one direction regardless of overprediction or underprediction, reducing numerical instability.",
        "MSE has gradient (prediction−actual)/n for each prediction because squaring cancels its own factor of two. Larger misses have weaker gradients after averaging and cannot destabilize model updates.",
        "For n examples, the derivative of MSE with respect to each predicted value is 2(prediction−actual)/n. Large errors create larger gradients, which can help correction but require care with outliers and learning rates."
      ],
      "explanation": "<strong>Explanation:</strong> MSE is smooth and differentiable, making it useful in gradient-based learning. For n predictions, its derivative with respect to each prediction is 2(prediction−actual)/n. Larger misses produce larger gradients and can drive stronger corrections, but outliers or excessive learning rates may destabilize updates. Imagine training a building power-use forecast: overpredictions and underpredictions produce gradients with opposite signs, guiding the optimizer to adjust model weights in a useful direction.",
      "figure": "<section class=\"mse-figure\"><span class=\"mse-kicker\">QUESTION 06 · VISUAL LESSON</span><div class=\"mse-art\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 440\" role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"mse-title-6 mse-desc-6\"><title id=\"mse-title-6\">A smooth bowl: bigger errors, bigger gradients</title><desc id=\"mse-desc-6\">Single-sample squared error is e squared. The derivative is 2e; at minus two and plus two the slopes are minus four and plus four.</desc><rect width=\"1000\" height=\"440\" fill=\"#f8fcfd\"/><text x=\"38\" y=\"39\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-size=\"14\" font-family=\"system-ui,Arial,sans-serif\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#278876\">06 · MEAN SQUARED ERROR</text><text x=\"38\" y=\"79\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-size=\"25\" font-family=\"system-ui,Arial,sans-serif\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#2e5368\">A smooth bowl: bigger errors, bigger gradients</text><rect x=\"34\" y=\"110\" width=\"608\" height=\"264\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#d5e5e9\" stroke-width=\"1.4\"/><line x1=\"106\" y1=\"367\" x2=\"585\" y2=\"367\" stroke=\"#7497a7\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><line x1=\"344\" y1=\"367\" x2=\"344\" y2=\"130\" stroke=\"#7497a7\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><line x1=\"106\" y1=\"367\" x2=\"585\" y2=\"367\" stroke=\"#e0eaf0\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><text x=\"96\" y=\"373\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-size=\"12\" font-family=\"system-ui,Arial,sans-serif\" font-weight=\"530\" fill=\"#758e9b\">0</text><line x1=\"106\" y1=\"340.8888888888889\" x2=\"585\" y2=\"340.8888888888889\" stroke=\"#e0eaf0\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><text x=\"96\" y=\"346.8888888888889\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-size=\"12\" font-family=\"system-ui,Arial,sans-serif\" font-weight=\"530\" fill=\"#758e9b\">1</text><line x1=\"106\" y1=\"262.55555555555554\" x2=\"585\" y2=\"262.55555555555554\" stroke=\"#e0eaf0\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><text x=\"96\" y=\"268.55555555555554\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-size=\"12\" font-family=\"system-ui,Arial,sans-serif\" font-weight=\"530\" fill=\"#758e9b\">4</text><line x1=\"106\" y1=\"132\" x2=\"585\" y2=\"132\" stroke=\"#e0eaf0\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><text x=\"96\" y=\"138\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-size=\"12\" font-family=\"system-ui,Arial,sans-serif\" font-weight=\"530\" fill=\"#758e9b\">9</text><path d=\"M105.0 132.0 L115.0 151.2 L125.0 169.5 L134.9 187.1 L144.9 203.8 L154.9 219.7 L164.9 234.8 L174.9 249.1 L184.8 262.6 L194.8 275.2 L204.8 287.0 L214.8 298.1 L224.8 308.3 L234.7 317.6 L244.7 326.2 L254.7 334.0 L264.7 340.9 L274.6 347.0 L284.6 352.3 L294.6 356.8 L304.6 360.5 L314.6 363.3 L324.5 365.4 L334.5 366.6 L344.5 367.0 L354.5 366.6 L364.5 365.4 L374.4 363.3 L384.4 360.5 L394.4 356.8 L404.4 352.3 L414.4 347.0 L424.3 340.9 L434.3 334.0 L444.3 326.2 L454.3 317.6 L464.3 308.3 L474.2 298.1 L484.2 287.0 L494.2 275.2 L504.2 262.6 L514.1 249.1 L524.1 234.8 L534.1 219.7 L544.1 203.8 L554.1 187.1 L564.0 169.5 L574.0 151.2 L584.0 132.0\" stroke=\"#248f9f\" stroke-width=\"5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><circle cx=\"184.83333333333331\" cy=\"262.55555555555554\" r=\"8\" fill=\"#df9c6d\"/><circle cx=\"504.1666666666667\" cy=\"262.55555555555554\" r=\"8\" fill=\"#df9c6d\"/><text x=\"126\" y=\"255\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-size=\"17\" font-family=\"system-ui,Arial,sans-serif\" font-weight=\"800\" fill=\"#b57a53\">e = −2</text><text x=\"488\" y=\"255\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-size=\"17\" font-family=\"system-ui,Arial,sans-serif\" font-weight=\"800\" fill=\"#b57a53\">e = +2</text><text x=\"344\" y=\"397\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"16\" font-family=\"system-ui,Arial,sans-serif\" font-weight=\"710\" fill=\"#597c8d\">prediction error e</text><rect x=\"671\" y=\"114\" width=\"295\" height=\"255\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#d5e5e9\" stroke-width=\"1.4\"/><text x=\"692\" y=\"151\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-size=\"17\" font-family=\"system-ui,Arial,sans-serif\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#578297\">GRADIENT</text><text x=\"693\" y=\"203\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-size=\"26\" font-family=\"system-ui,Arial,sans-serif\" font-weight=\"830\" fill=\"#346d7f\">Loss = e²</text><text x=\"693\" y=\"245\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-size=\"22\" font-family=\"system-ui,Arial,sans-serif\" font-weight=\"840\" fill=\"#268e83\">dLoss/de = 2e</text><text x=\"693\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-size=\"17\" font-family=\"system-ui,Arial,sans-serif\" font-weight=\"760\" fill=\"#af7957\">−2: gradient −4</text><text x=\"693\" y=\"320\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-size=\"17\" font-family=\"system-ui,Arial,sans-serif\" font-weight=\"760\" fill=\"#af7957\">+2: gradient +4</text></svg></div><p class=\"mse-note\">For MSE over n cases, dL/dprediction = 2(prediction−actual)/n.</p></section>"
    },
    {
      "stem": "How should MSE be evaluated and reported responsibly across time, groups, target scales, and production benchmarks while avoiding data leakage?",
      "tag": "Advanced · Honest benchmarking",
      "answer": "a",
      "options": [
        "Measure MSE on untouched, deployment-representative data using time- or group-aware splits when needed. Report squared target units, baseline performance, subgroup errors, and drift over time; compare scores only on compatible scales.",
        "Measure MSE on the same examples used for feature tuning because averaging squares removes leakage effects. Report global MSE without units, as its value does not change with the target measurement scale.",
        "Measure MSE on randomly mixed future and historical observations even when predicting tomorrow. Rescale each group differently, then compare the resulting scores directly to decide which group performs best.",
        "Measure MSE after optimizing the model with the final test set, then reuse those records for training. Squaring standardizes the result across target currencies and units, allowing universal score comparisons."
      ],
      "explanation": "<strong>Explanation:</strong> Evaluate MSE on data that did not influence model fitting or tuning. Respect time order for forecasting and group boundaries for related observations. Report squared target units, a baseline comparison, subgroup errors, and performance changes in production. For regional solar-power forecasts, one overall score might hide much larger errors in a particular region. MSE from targets with different scales or units is not directly comparable, and leakage can make test results look unrealistically good.",
      "figure": "<section class=\"mse-figure\"><span class=\"mse-kicker\">QUESTION 07 · VISUAL LESSON</span><div class=\"mse-art\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 440\" role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"mse-title-7 mse-desc-7\"><title id=\"mse-title-7\">A fair benchmark respects time, groups and units</title><desc id=\"mse-desc-7\">Chronological train validation and test, and group-specific MSE values in squared kWh, reveal reliability beyond one global score.</desc><rect width=\"1000\" height=\"440\" fill=\"#f8fcfd\"/><text x=\"38\" y=\"39\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-size=\"14\" font-family=\"system-ui,Arial,sans-serif\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#278876\">07 · MEAN SQUARED ERROR</text><text x=\"38\" y=\"79\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-size=\"25\" font-family=\"system-ui,Arial,sans-serif\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#2e5368\">A fair benchmark respects time, groups and units</text><rect x=\"32\" y=\"113\" width=\"931\" height=\"266\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#d5e5e9\" stroke-width=\"1.4\"/><text x=\"57\" y=\"152\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-size=\"17\" font-family=\"system-ui,Arial,sans-serif\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#527f91\">CHRONOLOGICAL EVALUATION</text><rect x=\"53\" y=\"176\" width=\"219\" height=\"80\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#ebf8fc\" stroke=\"#d4e5e9\" stroke-width=\"1.4\"/><text x=\"162.5\" y=\"225\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"18\" font-family=\"system-ui,Arial,sans-serif\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#3d8096\">2024 · TRAIN</text><rect x=\"285\" y=\"176\" width=\"216\" height=\"80\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#f3f4ff\" stroke=\"#d4e5e9\" stroke-width=\"1.4\"/><text x=\"393\" y=\"225\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"18\" font-family=\"system-ui,Arial,sans-serif\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#6473a2\">2025 · VALIDATE</text><rect x=\"516\" y=\"176\" width=\"412\" height=\"80\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#e9f9ef\" stroke=\"#d4e5e9\" stroke-width=\"1.4\"/><text x=\"722\" y=\"225\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"18\" font-family=\"system-ui,Arial,sans-serif\" font-weight=\"850\" fill=\"#2e8e79\">2026 · TEST · untouched</text><text x=\"56\" y=\"294\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-size=\"17\" font-family=\"system-ui,Arial,sans-serif\" font-weight=\"820\" fill=\"#5c8293\">SQUARED ERRORS ACROSS REGIONS (kWh²)</text><text x=\"56\" y=\"341\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-size=\"18\" font-family=\"system-ui,Arial,sans-serif\" font-weight=\"780\" fill=\"#507687\">Region A</text><rect x=\"165\" y=\"325\" width=\"192\" height=\"20\" rx=\"7\" fill=\"#e6f0f2\" stroke=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.4\"/><rect x=\"165\" y=\"325\" width=\"30.72\" height=\"20\" rx=\"7\" fill=\"#58b8a6\" stroke=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.4\"/><text x=\"368\" y=\"343\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-size=\"18\" font-family=\"system-ui,Arial,sans-serif\" font-weight=\"790\" fill=\"#3a7282\">4</text><text x=\"495\" y=\"341\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-size=\"18\" font-family=\"system-ui,Arial,sans-serif\" font-weight=\"780\" fill=\"#507687\">Region B</text><rect x=\"604\" y=\"325\" width=\"192\" height=\"20\" rx=\"7\" fill=\"#e6f0f2\" stroke=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.4\"/><rect x=\"604\" y=\"325\" width=\"192\" height=\"20\" rx=\"7\" fill=\"#e5a272\" stroke=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.4\"/><text x=\"807\" y=\"343\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-size=\"18\" font-family=\"system-ui,Arial,sans-serif\" font-weight=\"790\" fill=\"#3a7282\">25</text></svg></div><p class=\"mse-note\">Evaluation splits, squared units, baselines, groups and drift all matter.</p></section>"
